1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,609
One hidden door could let a stranger take over your web server.

2
00:00:03,659 --> 00:00:04,639
Meet Priya.

3
00:00:04,689 --> 00:00:10,002
Her online shop runs on Apache, one of the most popular web servers in the world.

4
00:00:10,052 --> 00:00:13,395
Every visitor talks to her server through HTTP/2.

5
00:00:13,445 --> 00:00:17,339
Think of it as one fast lane that carries lots of requests at once.

6
00:00:17,389 --> 00:00:22,466
The part of Apache that runs this lane is called mod_http2.

7
00:00:22,516 --> 00:00:24,246
And it had a memory bug.

8
00:00:24,296 --> 00:00:27,397
Send it the right messages, and the server gets confused.

9
00:00:27,447 --> 00:00:30,372
Then an attacker could run their own code on it.

10
00:00:30,422 --> 00:00:31,599
Wait a second...

11
00:00:31,649 --> 00:00:32,789
Hey!

12
00:00:32,839 --> 00:00:34,426
Not you too, Muja!

13
00:00:34,476 --> 00:00:42,312
Fun fact, buddy: the world's first web server, at CERN in 1990, had a sticker on it.

14
00:00:42,362 --> 00:00:44,615
It said, do not power it down!

15
00:00:44,665 --> 00:00:45,944
Okay, okay...

16
00:00:45,994 --> 00:00:47,412
back to our story.

17
00:00:47,462 --> 00:00:49,449
Apache rated it critical.

18
00:00:49,498 --> 00:00:57,196
Versions 2.4.55 to 2.4.70 are affected, when HTTP/2 is switched on.

19
00:00:57,246 --> 00:00:58,356
The fix is out.

20
00:00:58,406 --> 00:01:01,284
Priya updates to 2.4.71.

21
00:01:01,334 --> 00:01:03,216
Then she restarts the server.

22
00:01:03,266 --> 00:01:05,794
Yes, Muja, you can press it.

23
00:01:05,844 --> 00:01:08,426
The old code keeps running until she does.

24
00:01:08,476 --> 00:01:09,666
Can't patch today?

25
00:01:09,716 --> 00:01:12,658
Switch HTTP/2 off for now.

26
00:01:12,708 --> 00:01:17,048
No attacks were reported yet, but a public test lab already exists.

27
00:01:17,098 --> 00:01:18,647
So patch this week.

28
00:01:18,697 --> 00:01:21,088
The full story is on my blog.
